WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.132
Real datasets often contain missing values.

00:00:03.132 --> 00:00:10.357
In NumPy floating-point arrays, missing numeric information is commonly represented with N P dot nan.

00:00:10.357 --> 00:00:15.267
Notice what happens if we calculate the regular mean of an array containing nan.

00:00:15.267 --> 00:00:20.337
The result becomes nan because the missing value propagates through the calculation.

00:00:20.337 --> 00:00:26.547
Click Run and compare the regular mean with N P dot nanmean and N P dot nanmedian.

00:00:26.547 --> 00:00:31.022
The nan-aware functions ignore missing values during the calculation.

00:00:31.022 --> 00:00:39.167
The final part of the example replaces the missing value with the mean using N P dot nan

00:00:39.167 --> 00:00:42.097
to num. This is useful for teaching the mechanics, but in a real project,

00:00:42.097 --> 00:00:47.832
do not automatically replace every missing value with the mean.

00:00:47.832 --> 00:00:52.772
The correct treatment depends on why the data is missing, how much is missing,

00:00:52.772 --> 00:00:55.257
and what the analysis requires.

00:00:55.257 --> 00:00:59.456
First detect missingness. Then decide how it should be handled.
